Immutable factories and defensive copies.

A collection you hand to a caller is a shared, mutable object — and if you're not careful, they can change your internal state through it. This section is about controlling that: how to create collections that can't be modified, and how to defend the mutable ones at your boundaries. It's the collection-level version of the immutability discipline from records.

Since Java nine, each interface has static of-factories that build a small, unmodifiable collection in a single call. List of "red", "green", "blue". Set of "java", "jvm". Map of "red" to a colour code, "green" to another. These are perfect for constants and literals. Three things to know about them. First, they are truly immutable — any add, remove, set, or put throws an unsupported-operation exception. Second, they are null-hostile — a null element or key throws immediately, which is a deliberate design choice. And third, set-of and map-of reject duplicate elements or keys at construction, because a fixed literal shouldn't contain them. And for a snapshot of an existing collection, list-copy-of makes an immutable copy.

Now, a real distinction that's easy to confuse: an unmodifiable view versus an immutable copy. Suppose you have a live array-list. Collections dot unmodifiable-list wraps it in a read-only window onto that live list. Calling add on the view throws — you can't change it through the view. But here's the subtlety: if you change the original live list, that change is visible through the view, because the view is just a window. By contrast, list-copy-of makes an independent snapshot — change the original afterward, and the snapshot is completely unaffected, because it's a separate copy. So when you want a guarantee that nothing changes, take a copy, not a view.

This leads to the classic leak. A class stores a collection it was handed, or hands out its internal one. Either way, an outsider now holds a reference to your private state, and can mutate it behind your back. The fix is defensive copying. Take a team class that holds a list of members. In the constructor, you assign list-copy-of the argument — copying in — so that later edits to the caller's list don't touch you. And in the getter, since the field is already immutable, you can safely return it directly. So the rule is: copy in at the constructor, so the caller can't keep a back-door reference; and either copy out, or return an unmodifiable view, from your getters, so callers can't mutate your field. This is exactly the list-copy-of idiom from records, now applied to any class holding a collection.

So here's the habit. Prefer immutable collections wherever the contents are fixed — list-of for literals, copy-of for snapshots — because immutable objects are safe to share, cache, and use as map keys, with no defensive copying at all. And where you must hold a mutable collection, copy at the boundary: in on the way in, and out, or as a view, on the way out. Leaks of internal collections are one of the most common real-world bugs, and this one habit prevents them. Next: putting collections in order — comparable and comparator.
